        ":question/problem": "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,-\\frac{1}{2},-1)?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $\u2207g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-\\frac{1}{2},-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207g(x,y,z) & =\u27e8\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202z}(10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-6x,\\,-2y,\\,-3{z}^{2}\u27e9 \\\\ \u2207g(1,-\\frac{1}{2},-1) & =\u27e8-6(1),-2(-\\frac{1}{2}),-3(-1)^{2}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-6,1,-3\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $\u27e8-6,1,-3\u27e9.$"
      }
